PAGE MSG-SENDING
TITLE How a message is sent
KIND topic
SEE MSG-OUTBOX EMCON
BEGIN_TEXT
A message you compose stays "written" in the out box until the station
controller's result says it was keyed. Only "keyed" marks it sent.

When you answer y, the core hands your message to the station
controller over the station's link. The controller picks the channel,
claims the radio, and keys the burst. Its result comes back:

  keyed      The burst went on the air. The message moves to "sent"
             and waits for its acknowledgement.
  refused    The controller would not key it. The message is marked
             failed and shows the controller's reason.

The service decides which connection carries the message, and the
controller picks the channel. You see the channel on the y question,
read from the controller's status.

A message too long for one burst is refused before the y question
opens. The refusal names the size the controller takes on the
service's connection. The controller tells the core that size when it
accepts the connection; it is not always 2048 bytes. The core never
splits a message on this link.

EMCON keeps everything written: nothing goes on the air while EMCON
is on. Turn it off to let the queue go.
END
